"""
Swing-up and balancing of the Furuta pendulum by a nonlinear MPC alone.

An `MPCComponents.ACADOSMPC` solves, every tick, a finite-horizon optimal control problem over
the *nonlinear* multibody `QubePendulum` model itself and applies the first move: one real-time
iteration per tick over `Np` intervals of `Ts`, with the motor voltage bounded to ±`umax`, the arm
angle bounded to the end stops and the velocities bounded to what the model is trusted for (all
soft state bounds). There is no separate swing-up law and no switching: the same controller
swings the pendulum up from hanging and balances it.

The weighting is `design_lqr`'s -- state weight `Q1 = diag(1000, 10, 1, 1)`, `Q2 = 100`, terminal
cost the LQR cost-to-go of that design about upright (`terminal_lqr_cost`) -- plus one term that
makes it swing up: *energy shaping in the stage cost*. The pendulum's energy relative to the
upright's (rotation about the elbow plus the height of the centre of mass, the quantity the energy
swing-up pumps; `pendulum_energy_ratio` of the prediction model, 0 hanging at rest, 1 at rest
upright) is a fifth controlled output with the reference 1 and the weight `energy_weight`, which
makes the cost a nonlinear least squares (`ACADOSMPC`'s nonlinear outputs). From rest the term
gives the solver a gradient towards pumping from the first tick, which a terminal *set* on the
energy could not while the set was out of reach. Near upright the term vanishes quartically in the
deviation (the energy error is quadratic in the angle and the velocity), so the balancing is the
well-tried LQR: in simulation the closed loop from kicks up to 0.3 rad or 4 rad/s is identical to
the one without the term, to the voltage. The LQR weight on the arm still forbids the gentle
pumping, so the swing-up throws the arm past the end stops in 60 % of the rest starts (2.4 rad
median, up to 8.6 rad over 200 starts, see the README), as the terminal set did; a hard arm bound
removes the swing-up altogether.

Three details of the formulation matter for robustness, all found by simulating the loop with
quantized angles and the discrete velocity estimators:

  - The pendulum angle is wrapped to `[0, 2π)` (`AngleNormalization`) for the LQR part, whose
    reference is π. The wrap puts a 2π jump into the state at the bottom; a shifted warm start
    then fails once and `reset_on_failure` recovers it, a few extra solves per swing-up.
  - A single real-time iteration can produce an iterate the linearization no longer describes,
    after which every shifted warm start fails. Soft velocity bounds keep the iterates where
    the model is meaningful, and `reset_on_failure` retries a failed solve from a fresh guess.
  - The velocities are first differences through an exponential filter; at 10 ms a heavily
    filtered estimate (`velocity_filter` 0.5) makes even the balancing unstable, 0.8 to 1 is
    needed. The filter constant is runtime-settable in `FurutaMPCHardware`.

The prediction model is the very plant model: `furuta_mpc_dynamics()` (src/mpc.jl) compiles a
`QubePendulum` with `MultibodyComponents.multibody` and hands the result to
`continuous_dynamics` with the `ForwardDiff` Jacobian backend. A multibody model compiled this
way references cached linear solves (MTK diffcache parameters) that cannot be rebuilt
symbolically, which is what rules out the default `Symbolic` backend and, with it, C export --
this controller runs on the Julia solver backend only. The four MPC states are the model's
states, named so the state input does not depend on the compiler's state order: the two joint
angles and their derivatives, which are exactly what the hardware measures and what the
discrete `VelocityEstimator`s produce from the angles.
"""
component FurutaMPC
  "Measured arm (shoulder) angle"
  shoulder_angle = Dyad.RealInput() {^shoulder_angle}
  "Measured pendulum (elbow) angle, 0 hanging down"
  elbow_angle = Dyad.RealInput() {^elbow_angle}
  "Control command"
  u = Dyad.RealOutput() {^u}
  "acados status of the last MPC solve: 0 success, 2 maximum iterations, 4 QP failure, ..."
  exitflag = Dyad.RealOutput() {^exitflag}
  velocityestimator_shoulder = QuanserComponents.VelocityEstimator(final filter_param = velocity_filter) {^velocityestimator_shoulder}
  velocityestimator_elbow = QuanserComponents.VelocityEstimator(final filter_param = velocity_filter) {^velocityestimator_elbow}
  anglenormalization = QuanserComponents.AngleNormalization() {^anglenormalization}
  mpc = MPCComponents.ACADOSMPC(final dynamics = dynamics, final states = FURUTA_MPC_STATES, final outputs = FURUTA_MPC_OUTPUTS, final Ts = Ts, final Np = Np, final Q1 = furuta_mpc_weight(Q1, energy_weight), final Q2 = Q2, final umin = [-umax], final umax = [umax], final constrained = FURUTA_MPC_CONSTRAINED, final constrained_min = [-arm_limit, -velocity_limit_shoulder, -velocity_limit_elbow], final constrained_max = [arm_limit, velocity_limit_shoulder, velocity_limit_elbow], final soft_weight = soft_weight, final terminal_lqr_cost = true, final operating_point = [0, pi, 0, 0], final nlp_solver = nlp_solver, final warm_start = warm_start, final reset_on_failure = true, integrator = MPCComponents.ACADOSIntegrator.ERK(), integrator_stages = 2, final jacobian_backend = MPCComponents.ACADOSJacobianBackend.ForwardDiff(), final backend = MPCComponents.ACADOSBackend.Julia(), final max_iter = max_iter, final levenberg_marquardt = levenberg_marquardt, final qp_cond_N = qp_cond_N, final output_trajectories = output_trajectories, qp_solver = MPCComponents.ACADOSQPSolver.PartialCondensingHPIPM(), penalize_increments = false) {^mpc}
  zero = BlockComponents.Sources.Constant(final k = 0) {^zero}
  upright = BlockComponents.Sources.Constant(final k = pi) {^upright}
  level = BlockComponents.Sources.Constant(final k = 1) {^level}
  """
  Prediction model of the MPC: a `ContinuousDynamics` of the Furuta pendulum built with an
  AD Jacobian backend (see `furuta_mpc_dynamics`). The default is the `QubePendulum` with
  the identified parameters
  """
  structural parameter dynamics::Native = furuta_mpc_dynamics()
  "Sample time of the controller; the MPC's shooting interval, so it must equal the clock period"
  structural parameter Ts::Real = 0.01
  "Prediction horizon in shooting intervals; the horizon is Np * Ts"
  structural parameter Np::Integer = 60
  "Motor voltage bound of the MPC [V]. The swing-up needs 10 (clamp the command with `command_umax` instead of lowering this)"
  structural parameter umax::Real = 10.0
  "Arm angle the MPC keeps the arm within (softly, over its horizon) [rad]; inside the end stops at ±1.92. The swing-up under LQR weights violates it, see the component documentation"
  structural parameter arm_limit::Real = 1.7
  "Arm velocity the MPC keeps its predictions within [rad/s]; a soft bound that keeps the real-time iterates where the linearization is meaningful"
  structural parameter velocity_limit_shoulder::Real = 20.0
  "Pendulum velocity the MPC keeps its predictions within [rad/s]; see velocity_limit_shoulder"
  structural parameter velocity_limit_elbow::Real = 30.0
  "Stage weight on the pendulum's energy error (energy relative to the upright's minus 1), the swing-up term. Below 1e4 the swing-up is slow and wandering; 1e5 swings up from rest in about a second, and near upright the term is negligible (quartic in the deviation)"
  structural parameter energy_weight::Real = 1e5
  "Quadratic penalty on the slacks of the soft state bounds (arm angle and velocities)"
  structural parameter soft_weight::Real = 1e3
  "NLP solver: one real-time iteration per tick (SQP_RTI, the default) or SQP to convergence"
  structural parameter nlp_solver::MPCComponents.ACADOSSolver = MPCComponents.ACADOSSolver.SQP_RTI()
  "Initial guess of the NLP at every tick: Shift continues from the previous solution, which the swing-up over the horizon needs (with reset_on_failure protecting it); None restarts from the current state and cannot swing up"
  structural parameter warm_start::MPCComponents.ACADOSWarmStart = MPCComponents.ACADOSWarmStart.Shift()
  "Maximum SQP iterations per tick (SQP only). Structural here, since acados sizes its memory by it"
  structural parameter max_iter::Integer = 30
  "Levenberg-Marquardt regularization of the Gauss-Newton Hessian"
  structural parameter levenberg_marquardt::Real = 1.0
  "Horizon of HPIPM's partially condensed QP; 5 halves the worst-case solve time of the full horizon (-1) and keeps the iterates tamer"
  structural parameter qp_cond_N::Integer = 5
  "Record the MPC's predicted trajectories, KKT residuals and iteration count at every tick (`mpc.x_pred`, `mpc.u_pred`, `mpc.residuals`, `mpc.iterations`), what `MPCComponents.mpc_gui` shows"
  structural parameter output_trajectories::Boolean = false
  "Stage weight on the deviation from upright in the order [shoulder_angle, elbow_angle, shoulder_velocity, elbow_velocity]: `design_lqr`'s state weight, so the balancing is the well-tried LQR. Also defines the terminal cost-to-go. The MPC's stage weight is this matrix extended with `energy_weight` (see `furuta_mpc_weight`)"
  structural parameter Q1::Real[4, 4] = diagonal([1000.0, 10.0, 1.0, 1.0])
  "Control weight: `design_lqr`'s"
  structural parameter Q2::Real[1, 1] = diagonal([100.0])
  "Exponential filter constant of the velocity estimators (1 = unfiltered first difference). 0.5 makes the balancing unstable at 10 ms; 0.8 leaves some smoothing of the encoder quantization"
  parameter velocity_filter::Real = 0.8
relations
  connect(elbow_angle, velocityestimator_elbow.pos, anglenormalization.u) {^id1}
  connect(anglenormalization.y, mpc.x[2]) {^id2}
  connect(shoulder_angle, velocityestimator_shoulder.pos, mpc.x[1]) {^id3}
  connect(velocityestimator_shoulder.vel, mpc.x[3]) {^id4}
  connect(velocityestimator_elbow.vel, mpc.x[4]) {^id5}
  # The reference is the upright state: arm centred, pendulum at π, at rest, and the energy of rest upright.
  connect(zero.y, mpc.r[1], mpc.r[3], mpc.r[4]) {^id6}
  connect(upright.y, mpc.r[2]) {^id7}
  connect(level.y, mpc.r[5]) {^id10}
  connect(mpc.u[1], u) {^id8}
  connect(mpc.exitflag, exitflag) {^id9}
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://QuanserComponents/SwingupCatch.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "shoulder_angle": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": -100, "x2": 0, "y1": 150, "y2": 250}}}
    },
    "elbow_angle": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": -100, "x2": 0, "y1": 450, "y2": 550}}}
    },
    "u": {"Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 1000, "x2": 1100, "y1": 450, "y2": 550}}}},
    "exitflag": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 1000, "x2": 1100, "y1": 150, "y2": 250}}}
    },
    "velocityestimator_shoulder": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 100, "x2": 200, "y1": 250, "y2": 350}}}
    },
    "velocityestimator_elbow": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 100, "x2": 200, "y1": 550, "y2": 650}}}
    },
    "anglenormalization": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 100, "x2": 200, "y1": 400, "y2": 500}}}
    },
    "mpc": {"Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 450, "x2": 550, "y1": 300, "y2": 400}}}},
    "zero": {"Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 250, "x2": 350, "y1": 50, "y2": 150}}}},
    "upright": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 250, "x2": 350, "y1": 150, "y2": 250}}}
    }
  }
}
end

"""
The MPC controller closed around the physical QUBE, with the hardware I/O inside the
synchronous program -- `FurutaHardware` with `FurutaMPC` in place of the swing-up state
machine.

`HardwareMeasurement` reads the encoders, `HardwareCommand` writes the motor and the
`DataLogger` appends a row per tick in the `MPC_LOG_COLUMNS` order: what was measured and
applied, the loop diagnostics, and acados' `exitflag` of every solve. Compiling this model
yields a synchronous program that needs nothing from its caller but a clock tick; see
`generate_mpc_controller` and `MPCController` in src/mpc.jl.

Unlike `FurutaHardware` there is no homing and no error recovery: the arm limit is a
constraint of the MPC instead, and the arm starts from wherever it is (`open_hardware!`'s
`arm_deg` says where that is). `command_umax` clamps the command before the amplifier and is
runtime-settable, the one `TuningGains` field, so a first run can be made with less voltage
than the MPC is allowed to plan with.

The model can also be run against the device as a *simulation*, i.e. stepped by an ODE solver
rather than compiled to a program -- that is how the MPC's predicted trajectories and solver
residuals end up in a solution object for `MPCComponents.mpc_gui`. Two options exist for
that: `realtime = true` makes `HardwareDiagnostics` pace the ticks on the wall clock, and
`output_trajectories = true` makes the MPC record them. `run_mpc_hardware_model` in
src/mpc.jl does the run.
"""
component FurutaMPCHardware
  measurement = HardwareMeasurement() {^measurement}
  control_system = FurutaMPC(final dynamics = dynamics, final Ts = Ts, final Np = Np, final umax = umax, final arm_limit = arm_limit, final nlp_solver = nlp_solver, final warm_start = warm_start, final output_trajectories = output_trajectories, final velocity_filter = velocity_filter) {^control_system}
  command = HardwareCommand(final umax = command_umax) {^command}
  diagnostics = HardwareDiagnostics(final realtime = realtime) {^diagnostics}
  logger = DataLogger(final n = MPC_LOG_NCOLS, final filename = log_file, final header = MPC_LOG_HEADER) {^logger}
  periodicclock = DiscreteComponents.PeriodicClock(final dt = Ts) {^periodicclock}
  "Controller sample time"
  structural parameter Ts::Real = 0.01
  "Prediction horizon in shooting intervals"
  structural parameter Np::Integer = 60
  "Prediction model of the MPC (see `furuta_mpc_dynamics`)"
  structural parameter dynamics::Native = furuta_mpc_dynamics()
  "Motor voltage bound of the MPC [V]; the swing-up needs 10. `command_umax` clamps what reaches the amplifier"
  structural parameter umax::Real = 10.0
  "Arm angle the MPC keeps the arm within [rad], inside the end stops at ±1.92"
  structural parameter arm_limit::Real = 1.7
  "NLP solver of the MPC"
  structural parameter nlp_solver::MPCComponents.ACADOSSolver = MPCComponents.ACADOSSolver.SQP_RTI()
  "Initial guess of the MPC's NLP at every tick (see `FurutaMPC`)"
  structural parameter warm_start::MPCComponents.ACADOSWarmStart = MPCComponents.ACADOSWarmStart.Shift()
  "File the log is written to; the driver opens it with this name"
  structural parameter log_file::String = MPC_LOG_FILE
  "Pace the ticks in real time from inside the program: for running this model as a *simulation* (an ODE solver stepping it) against the device, see `run_mpc_hardware_model`. Off when `run_program!`'s loop keeps time"
  structural parameter realtime::Boolean = false
  "Record the MPC's predicted trajectories and solver residuals at every tick, for `MPCComponents.mpc_gui`"
  structural parameter output_trajectories::Boolean = false
  "Saturation applied to the command before it is written to the amplifier [V]. Runtime-settable, a `TuningGains` field"
  parameter command_umax::Dyad.Voltage = umax
  "Exponential filter constant of the velocity estimators (1 = unfiltered). Runtime-settable, a `TuningGains` field"
  parameter velocity_filter::Real = 0.8
relations
  connect(measurement.shoulder_angle, control_system.shoulder_angle, logger.u[2], periodicclock.y) {^id7}
  connect(measurement.elbow_angle, control_system.elbow_angle, logger.u[3]) {^id8}
  connect(control_system.u, command.u) {^id9}
  # The applied voltage is the diagnostics' dependency token as well as a logged column:
  # `exec` is latched by the write, so the reads have to be scheduled after it.
  connect(command.u_applied, diagnostics.dep, logger.u[4]) {^id10}
  connect(diagnostics.elapsed, logger.u[1]) {^id11}
  connect(diagnostics.dt, logger.u[5]) {^id12}
  connect(diagnostics.exec, logger.u[6]) {^id13}
  connect(control_system.exitflag, logger.u[7]) {^id14}
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://QuanserComponents/FurutaHardware.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "measurement": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 20, "x2": 120, "y1": 136, "y2": 236}}}
    },
    "control_system": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 160, "x2": 260, "y1": 140, "y2": 240}}}
    },
    "command": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 300, "x2": 400, "y1": 140, "y2": 240}}}
    },
    "diagnostics": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 440, "x2": 540, "y1": 140, "y2": 240}}}
    },
    "logger": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 790, "x2": 890, "y1": 140, "y2": 240}}}
    },
    "periodicclock": {"Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 20, "x2": 120, "y1": 10, "y2": 110}}}},
    "id7": {},
    "id8": {},
    "id9": {},
    "id10": {},
    "id11": {},
    "id12": {},
    "id13": {},
    "id14": {}
  }
}
end

"""
The MPC controller closed around the simulated `QubePendulum` -- `FurutaSwingup` with
`FurutaMPC` in place of the swing-up state machine. The plant and the MPC's prediction
model are the same multibody model with the identified parameters.

Compile with `multibody(model, additional_passes = [SynchToolkit.compile_lustre])` and
simulate with `dt = Ts`, as in the README.
"""
component FurutaMPCSwingup
  control_system = FurutaMPC(final Ts = Ts) {^control_system}
  qubependulum = QuanserComponents.QubePendulum(idparams = QuanserComponents.identified) {^qubependulum}
  zeroorderhold = DiscreteComponents.ZeroOrderHold(initial_condition = 0) {^zeroorderhold}
  elbow_sampler = DiscreteComponents.SampleWithADEffects(quantized = false, sigma = 0.00001) {^elbow_sampler}
  shoulder_sampler = DiscreteComponents.SampleWithADEffects(quantized = false, sigma = 0.00001) {^shoulder_sampler}
  periodicclock = DiscreteComponents.PeriodicClock(final dt = Ts) {^periodicclock}
  world = MultibodyComponents.World(default_body_color = gray, default_rod_color = gray, default_joint_color = gray, nominal_length = 0.1, render = false) {^world}
  parameter gray::Real[4] = [0.9, 0.9, 0.9, 1]
  "Controller sample time"
  structural parameter Ts::Real = 0.01
relations
  connect(elbow_sampler.y, control_system.elbow_angle) {^id8}
  connect(shoulder_sampler.y, control_system.shoulder_angle, periodicclock.y) {^id9}
  connect(qubependulum.shoulder_angle, shoulder_sampler.u) {^id10}
  connect(zeroorderhold.y, qubependulum.voltage) {^id11}
  connect(qubependulum.elbow_angle, elbow_sampler.u) {^id12}
  connect(control_system.u, zeroorderhold.u) {^id13}
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://QuanserComponents/FurutaSwingup.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "control_system": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 200, "x2": 300, "y1": 300, "y2": 400}}}
    },
    "qubependulum": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 600, "x2": 700, "y1": 300, "y2": 400}}}
    },
    "zeroorderhold": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 400, "x2": 500, "y1": 300, "y2": 400}}}
    },
    "elbow_sampler": {
      "Dyad": {
        "placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 400, "x2": 500, "y1": 550, "y2": 650, "rot": 180}}
      }
    },
    "shoulder_sampler": {
      "Dyad": {
        "placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 400, "x2": 500, "y1": 100, "y2": 200, "rot": 180}}
      }
    },
    "periodicclock": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 200, "x2": 300, "y1": 100, "y2": 200}}}
    },
    "world": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 600, "x2": 700, "y1": 100, "y2": 200}}}
    },
    "id8": {},
    "id9": {},
    "id10": {},
    "id11": {},
    "id12": {},
    "id13": {}
  }
}
end